@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="th">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    @* width=device-width + initial-scale=1 is what makes the mobile CSS in theme.css actually apply -
       without it, phones render the page at a fake ~980px "desktop" viewport and zoom out, which
       defeats every @@media breakpoint in the stylesheet. viewport-fit=cover is what lets the page
       draw into the notch/home-indicator area at all; without it the env(safe-area-inset-*) padding
       in theme.css always resolves to 0 and iOS just letterboxes the app in black bars instead. *@
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover" />

    @* ---- Installable web app -------------------------------------------------------------------
       This console is checked from a phone, over Tailscale, usually in a hurry ("is COM-25 still
       playing games?"). A manifest + these Apple meta tags mean it can be added to the home screen
       and opened as a standalone app: its own icon, no browser URL bar eating 15% of a small screen,
       and it lands directly on the Clients page. No service worker on purpose - every byte on this
       page is live lab state that must never be served from a cache, and an offline shell for an app
       that is useless without the tailnet would only produce convincing-looking stale screens. *@
    <link rel="manifest" href="manifest.webmanifest" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#241E1A" />
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes" />
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style" content="black-translucent" />
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-title" content="OnionConsole" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="img/logo-256.png" />
    <link rel="icon" href="img/logo-128.png" />

    <base href="/" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/theme.css" />

    @* Applies the saved theme BEFORE the first paint. This has to be an inline, synchronous script in
       <head>: a Blazor Server circuit doesn't exist yet this early, so doing it in C# would flash the
       light theme on every load before switching. Kept to the smallest possible body - the real logic
       lives in js/theme.js, which the toggle imports later. *@
    <script>
        (function () {
            try {
                var m = localStorage.getItem('onion.theme');
                if (m === 'dark' || m === 'light') { document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', m); }
                var dark = m === 'dark' || (m !== 'light' && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches);
                document.querySelector('meta[name="theme-color"]').setAttribute('content', dark ? '#0F0D0C' : '#241E1A');
            } catch (e) { /* private mode: fall through to the OS preference, which theme.css handles on its own */ }
        })();
    </script>

    <HeadOutlet />
</head>
<body>
    @* Thin navigation progress bar. Sits outside the Blazor render tree on purpose - app.js shows and
       hides it, so it can appear the instant a nav link is tapped rather than after the server has
       re-rendered (which is the very thing being waited for). *@
    <div class="nav-progress" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    @* ---- Reconnect overlay -------------------------------------------------------------------
       Blazor injects its own "Rejoining the server..." dialog when the circuit drops - a hard-coded
       white card with an English string, which on a dark-themed Thai console looks like a different
       application briefly took over the screen. Defining an element with this exact id switches Blazor
       to its user-dialog code path, where it only toggles classes on our markup instead of building
       its own.

       The exact state machine, read out of blazor.web.js rather than guessed (class `Go`):
         show()      -> clears all, adds -show
         update()    -> adds -retrying ON TOP of -show, and fills the attempt/seconds spans by id
         pause()     -> REMOVES -show and -retrying, adds -paused
         failed()    -> adds -failed, or -resume-failed if it was resuming rather than reconnecting
         rejected()  -> adds -rejected
         hide()      -> clears all, adds -hide
       The two removal cases are why -paused and -resume-failed must be styled: a first version handled
       only show/failed/rejected, so a paused circuit would have hidden the overlay completely and left
       the user staring at a frozen page with nothing to explain it. *@
    <div id="components-reconnect-modal" class="reconnect-overlay">
        <div class="reconnect-card">
            <div class="reconnect-spinner" aria-hidden="true"></div>

            <div class="reconnect-body reconnect-when-show">
                <div class="reconnect-title">กำลังเชื่อมต่อใหม่...</div>
                <div class="reconnect-detail">
                    การเชื่อมต่อกับเซิร์ฟเวอร์หลุดไป กำลังลองใหม่
                    (ครั้งที่ <span id="components-reconnect-current-attempt">1</span>
                    จาก <span id="components-reconnect-max-retries">30</span>)
                </div>
                @* Blazor fills this in only while it is counting down between attempts. *@
                <div class="reconnect-detail reconnect-countdown">
                    ลองใหม่อีกครั้งในอีก <span id="components-seconds-to-next-attempt">0</span> วินาที
                </div>
                <div class="reconnect-detail">ถ้าเพิ่งสลับ WiFi หรือออกนอกวง Tailscale ให้ต่อกลับเข้าไปก่อน</div>
            </div>

            <div class="reconnect-body reconnect-when-paused">
                <div class="reconnect-title">พักการเชื่อมต่อไว้ก่อน</div>
                <div class="reconnect-detail">หยุดลองเชื่อมต่อใหม่ชั่วคราว กดปุ่มด้านล่างเพื่อต่อเดี๋ยวนี้</div>
                <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="window.Blazor.reconnect()">เชื่อมต่อเดี๋ยวนี้</button>
            </div>

            <div class="reconnect-body reconnect-when-failed">
                <div class="reconnect-title">เชื่อมต่อใหม่ไม่สำเร็จ</div>
                <div class="reconnect-detail">เช็คว่าอุปกรณ์นี้ยังต่อ Tailscale อยู่ แล้วลองอีกครั้ง</div>
                <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="window.Blazor.reconnect()">ลองเชื่อมต่ออีกครั้ง</button>
            </div>

            <div class="reconnect-body reconnect-when-resume-failed">
                <div class="reconnect-title">กลับเข้าเซสชันเดิมไม่ได้</div>
                <div class="reconnect-detail">เซสชันบนเซิร์ฟเวอร์หายไปแล้ว ต้องโหลดหน้าใหม่เพื่อเริ่มใหม่</div>
                <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="location.reload()">โหลดหน้าใหม่</button>
            </div>

            <div class="reconnect-body reconnect-when-rejected">
                <div class="reconnect-title">เซสชันหมดอายุ</div>
                <div class="reconnect-detail">เซิร์ฟเวอร์ปิดการเชื่อมต่อเดิมไปแล้ว ต้องโหลดหน้าใหม่</div>
                <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="location.reload()">โหลดหน้าใหม่</button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <Routes />
    <script src="_framework/blazor.web.js"></script>
    @* App-shell behaviour (nav drawer + theme switching) lives in plain JS on purpose - the layout
       that hosts those controls is rendered statically under this app's per-page interactivity model,
       so C# @@onclick handlers in it can never fire. Full reasoning at the top of app.js. Loaded after
       blazor.web.js and bound with delegated document listeners, so it survives enhanced navigation. *@
    <script src="js/app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
